{"archetypes":[{"name":"bash32-compat","focus_area":"correctness","weight":1,"rationale":"AGENTS.md strictly prohibits Bash 4+ constructs; the new driver and harness use arrays, local -a, printf -v, and unset-safe expansions that need Bash 3.2 verification.","prompt_body":"Audit every new or modified shell script in this diff (design-plan-quality-assessor.sh, test-design-plan-quality-assessor.sh, and the SKILL.md inline fence) for Bash 3.2 portability violations. Focus on: `local -a _kvs=()` initialization, `${WARN_LINES[@]+\"${WARN_LINES[@]}\"}` unset-safe array expansion (valid in 3.2?), any `[[ ]]` constructs that require Bash 4+, `printf -v varname` availability, and whether `phase_driver_write_result_env` or `emit_kv` use any Bash 4+ builtins. Also check the `json_scalar_or_sed` sed-regex interpolation of `$key` for shell-quoting safety when the key contains regex metacharacters. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"driver-protocol","focus_area":"correctness","weight":1,"rationale":"The driver/orchestrator protocol has three interlocking contracts (file-first env parse, two-step WARN= replay, and three-guard abort block) that must be byte-stable with the Step 2b postplan fence; any mismatch silently mis-routes WORSE Continue/Stop or suppresses operator warnings.","prompt_body":"Review the correctness of the driver/orchestrator handoff protocol across skills/design/SKILL.md Step 3.6 fence and skills/design/scripts/design-plan-quality-assessor.sh. Verify: (1) the file-first parse loop sets _assessor_parse_ok=true on any allowlisted key match (not just on the first key), so a file with only WARN= lines still suppresses stdout WARN replay; (2) the stdout merge loop correctly uses fill-only-unset (`-z \"${!_assessor_key:-}\"`) rather than unconditional overwrite; (3) the three abort guards (rc=2, rc=0+empty ASSESSOR_STATUS, rc not in {0,2}) fire in the right order and cover the case where the driver writes a result env but exits non-zero; (4) the non-HARD path unconditionally calls the driver after printing the skip breadcrumb — confirm this does not produce a double skip message or an unexpected second invocation artifact. Also check whether `emit_kv` in the driver writes to FD3 (quiet stream) vs stdout and whether `$()` capture in the SKILL.md fence would miss FD3 output when LARCH_QUIET_DISABLE is not set. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"pause-abort-ordering","focus_area":"code-robustness","weight":1,"rationale":"The _assessor_pause_checkpoint calls fail() before writing the result env when ISSUE_NUMBER cannot be resolved, violating the plan contract that says 'write ASSESSOR_STATUS=skipped first, then exec design-pause-save.sh'.","prompt_body":"Examine the _assessor_pause_checkpoint function in skills/design/scripts/design-plan-quality-assessor.sh for the ordering of the ISSUE_NUMBER resolution guard versus the _write_result_and_emit call. The plan contract states the result env should be written with ASSESSOR_STATUS=skipped before exec-ing design-pause-save.sh; verify whether the `[[ -n \"$_issue\" ]] || fail ...` guard aborts (exit 2) before or after the write, and what the orchestrator abort block does when it sees rc=2 from a pause-triggered invocation. Also check whether the second _assessor_pause_checkpoint call (after _read_round_cursor) could trigger a pause after the read-cursor succeeded but before write-after, and whether ROUND_NUM is correctly populated in the result env at that point. Examine whether the exit-2 from the pause checkpoint abort causes the orchestrator to emit a config-error banner when the true cause is a missing ISSUE_NUMBER during pause — this would be misleading to the operator. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."}]}
